WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.850
So Mexico City is a place

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:07.520
of paradoxes, of conundrums,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.170
and of water that it's
there but you don't see it.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.350
There's parts of the cities
where you would never know

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.640
just by looking at them that there's any

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.520
water crisis around.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.490
They have clean water 24 hours a day,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.470
apparently just as abundant as it would be

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.230
in New York or something like that.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.060
Then you have other parts of the city

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.290
where people are living

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.610
in very, very, extremely
precarious water situations.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.357
Low-income parts of Mexico City

00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:34.060
and the people that live around it

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.870
are now facing a situation
where they open the tap

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.460
and no water comes out.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.720
It's a very tragic story of having

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.350
all of the water in the
world and somehow ending up

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.250
being a city that today is on the list

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.360
of top 10 cities most
likely to run out of water.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.340
You have to do something, and so we do.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.180
Enrique Lomnitz' hometown, Mexico City,

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.940
is in a water crisis that
gets worse by the day.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.740
He started social enterprise Isla Urbana

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.550
to stem the tide.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.940
They design and build
cheap and simple systems

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.070
that catch and clean rainwater.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.990
There's nothing new about
rainwater harvesting.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.120
People have been harvesting rainwater

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.640
since they first opened their mouths

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.350
and looked up at the sky at the same time.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.120
It seemed to us like a logical thing.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.190
Just figure out how we can use this water

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.420
that we have available to us

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.887
as kind of like a first
part of a larger solution.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.610
It's a situation that affects
women very particularly.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.280
Mostly it's women who tend to be more

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.660
kind of in charge of
managing the household

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.480
and basically their lives start circling

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.923
around these water issues.

00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:04.260
Elizabeth is one of 11 people

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.060
living in this house on
the edge of Mexico City.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.487
She's also one of 2.5 million people

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:12.763
who don't have access to
permanent and safe drinking water.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:28.170
But going thirsty isn't where Elizabeth

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.960
and her family's problems end.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.490
The economic implications
of not having water

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:33.870
are very interesting,
and the people that rely

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.530
on water trucks, for example,

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.060
they often have to have somebody at home

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.850
all the time to wait for a
possible water truck arrival

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.460
so there's often someone
that can't work at all.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.770
They just need to always be at home.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.830
What's strange about Mexico City, though,

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.975
is it isn't some barren desert.

00:02:50.975 --> 00:02:53.427
It's wet. Really wet.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.140
Here we'll have one hour

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.180
where it's like apocalyptic.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.980
The sky is just falling and
there's lighting and thunder

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.660
and rivers in the streets.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.284
During the rainy season
you will have floods.

00:03:08.284 --> 00:03:11.170
Those floods can last for days.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.420
Mexico City gets considerably more rain

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.820
than a city like London does, for example.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.510
The question that comes to one's mind

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:22.160
is if we have so much water resources,

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.090
why do we have to be in
such a bad situation?

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.270
The water crisis in Mexico
City is a 400-year-old story.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:33.260
Mexico City started off
being a city on an island

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.720
in the center of a lake in this basin

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.380
high up in the mountains.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.930
And over the course of 400 years,

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.360
basically the city has focused on draining

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.550
all of these ancient
lakes out of the aquifer

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.750
and building a giant
modern city in its place.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.400
So where you had this
2,000-square-kilometer lake

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.730
you now have just city as
far as the eye can see.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.650
You see a lot of highways
and a lot of avenues

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.260
and streets who have the name of rivers.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.260
Those streets were actually
rivers 50, 60 years ago.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.880
To provide for its vast population

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.070
around 1,800 wells pump
water out of the ground,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.880
depleting the aquifer below.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.410
But because the city's
prone to deadly flooding

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.990
it's built a massive infrastructure

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.150
to get the water out
quickly when it rains.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.650
The result: not enough water filters down

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.103
to recharge the aquifer.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.320
The loss of that water
that you can actually see

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:35.000
is paralleled with the drying
up of all of the aquifers.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.300
We're just pumping and
pumping and pumping water

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.220
out of the ground to the
degree that, actually,

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.050
Mexico City is physically sinking.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:44.620
More than 10 meters in the last century.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.560
Monuments need steps added
for people to reach them,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.920
streets rise and fall,
sinkholes open up the earth,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.640
and the water from the ground isn't enough

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.200
to quench the city's thirst.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:57.563
30% of its water is imported
from outside the city.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.670
But the infrastructure is so old and huge

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.640
not only is 40% of water
lost through leaks,

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.030
but much of what does make
it can become contaminated.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.540
Many people end up spending
a significant portion

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.003
of their income on
expensive bottled water.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.790
People don't trust the
quality of the water

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.240
they get out of the grid even in areas

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.540
where it's actually quite good.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.910
98 or so percent of the population
doesn't drink tap water.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:29.610
They buy water from purifiers,
from these filter stores

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.243
that will fill your five-gallon jugs.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.230
We're industrial designers, and honestly,

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.070
we were looking for problems to solve.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.360
People just started
telling us about water.

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:42.020
They started telling us about how

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.760
their water situation was
getting worse and worse.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.950
Isla Urbana installed their first system

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:50.780
here in Ajusco on the
southern edge of the city.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.470
It didn't take long for people
to realize the benefits.

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.670
Little by little almost
all of the neighbors

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:59.710
have started harvesting
rainwater themselves.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:00.850
We're working with communities

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:03.110
so that they can become self-reliant

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:04.640
in getting their own water supply

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:06.670
and securing their own water.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.660
It needs to be as simple as possible.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:10.510
Rain falls on the roof,
goes through the gutters,

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.810
goes through the pipe,
diverts the first volume,

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:15.020
goes into a tank, gets chlorinated,

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:17.430
you let the little bits of dust settle

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.690
and then you pump that water out

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:20.960
and give it just some simple filtration.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.690
That water you can just connect directly

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.760
into your house's existing plumbing system

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.610
so you open a tap in the kitchen

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.960
and that's the rainwater
that's coming out.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.090
For families in these areas that are very,

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.480
very water-stressed,
starting to harvest rainwater

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:36.970
can mean sometimes doubling
the amount of water

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.140
that you actually have available.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:42.170
95% or so of the water
that's used in a household

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.840
is not for drinking,
it's for everything else,

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.050
and so that's really
where you wanna hit first

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:47.960
'cause that's where you're gonna make

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.410
the biggest first difference.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.540
So we want to have our baseline system

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:54.780
be as affordable as possible,
but designed in such a way

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.680
that you can add new
treatment systems to it

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.040
so that you can eventually stop

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.010
having to buy drinking water as well.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:04.820
The water, it's water,
except it's just water

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.860
that's reaching your house via
falling on you from the sky

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.310
instead of coming from
the city's water grid.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.470
We get usually between
700 and 1,000 liters

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:14.410
of water per year for
every square meter of roof.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:15.710
So if you have a house that has, say,

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.483
a 100-square-meter roof,
it's not a huge house at all,

00:08:20.483 --> 00:08:22.150
a house like that can get up to

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:24.450
around 100,000 liters of water per year.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.970
The problem at that point becomes

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.050
they just don't have enough
tanks to store it all.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.460
Mexico City should be able to get

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.840
at least 20 to 30% of its water demand

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.810
through rainwater harvesting systems,

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.720
which is very significant.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:39.450
It's an incredible amount of water.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.380
Guaranteeing water goes way beyond

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.640
quenching thirst and saving money.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.130
It could also save a city from itself.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:48.330
You have health, economic,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.090
social, political problems.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.600
If you divide the amount of water

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:59.130
that gets into the network every second,

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.800
every inhabitant of Mexico City

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.120
should get 350 liters per day.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.520
If the entire eastern part of Mexico City

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:09.960
runs out of water there's gonna be

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.780
incredible amounts of social friction

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.980
and discontent and political crises.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.903
We all live in this valley.
We all need to be okay.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:19.500
The local government has recognized

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.280
the perilous position
the city finds itself in

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.710
and last year invested in
10,000 of Isla Urbana's systems.

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:29.530
They plan to install 100,000 by 2024.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.850
Many believe regenerating areas of nature

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.790
holds the key to protecting cities

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:01.163
from problems like water sustainability.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.330
We're not going to be able to return

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:08.880
to the Aztec city in
the middle of the lake.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.080
That's gone.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.680
But we can have a new
water model for the city.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:19.030
A model where we keep
water, we have rivers,

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:24.030
we have small lakes, and water
can be a friend and an ally

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.523
and not a foe like it's today.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:31.770
When it comes to running out of water,

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.840
Mexico City isn't alone.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.770
L.A., Cape Town, Sao
Paulo, the list is growing,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.400
each with millions of inhabitants

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:41.640
facing devastating water crises.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.100
They're serving as a warning

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.350
as more of the world's population
migrates to urban centers.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.820
Mexico City in 1940

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.230
had maybe a 1.5 million inhabitants

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.520
and today has close to 23 million people.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.130
And this incredibly violent growth

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.730
and all this incredibly
fast expansion of the city

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.180
has put unbelievable stress
on all of the natural systems

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:04.610
that provide us with things like water.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.340
Since 2009 Isla Urbana have installed

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.790
more than 20,000 of
their harvesting systems,

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:12.680
mostly on top of low-income
homes in Mexico City

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:14.030
and in rural communities.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:17.440
Their devices catch a combined
800 million liters of water

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.140
each year, providing some 120,000 people

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.913
with a significant portion
of their water needs.

00:11:26.701 --> 00:11:27.740
I believe a lot in the

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.313
empowerment aspect of this.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:32.910
How do we transition to being a society

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:35.380
where everybody is actively participating

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.580
in water management and
is actually harvesting

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.210
their own water and taking care of it

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.837
and not relying on this other system?

